            <ArticleTitle>Off-label prescribing: in search of a reasonable patient-centered approach.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>131-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/15360288.2012.679335</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This commentary relates to an article entitled &quot;Off-Label Prescribing of Medications for Pain: Maintaining Optimal Care at an Intersection of Law, Public Policy, and Ethics,&quot; which appears in the same journal issue. Off-label prescribing and off-label marketing of drugs are differentiated. Vulnerability of pain patients with off-label prescribing is noted as is the lack of data on benefits and risks of the practice. The influence of the pharmaceutical industry in off-label prescribing is discussed. A lack of transparency when communicating with patients about off-label drug use is a concern.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
